<h1 style=3D"font-size:28px;line-height:1.2;">Designing for small screens</h1>
<p>Hi Anna,</p>
<p>This week, we=E2=80=99re looking at the details that make a small screen f=
eel less cramped=E2=80=94and a little more human.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Put navigation within reach</h2>
<p>Thumb-friendly navigation starts with comfortable placement, generous touc=
h targets, and enough space between actions. Try your layout with one hand: t=
he awkward stretches often reveal more than a tidy desktop preview.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Give captions room to breathe</h2>
<p>Readable captions need contrast, clear wording, and a size that holds up o=
n a phone. Keep them close to the image they describe without squeezing them =
into the margins.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">In conversation with Nela Brandt</h2>
<p>Our featured interview explores the illustrator=E2=80=99s approach to visu=
al storytelling, with an eye toward how images and their accompanying words w=
ork together.</p>
<p>What small-screen detail do you wish more designers paid attention to? Rep=
ly and tell us.</p>
